 ## Subtopics
 
-### Object-Aligned Slicing
-Object-aligned slicing divides the volume into slices along the principal axes (x, y, z) rather than the view direction [S1, unofficial]. It uses three stacks of 2D textures corresponding to these directions. The active stack is swapped based on the viewpoint's closest principal direction [S1, unofficial]. It is faster and simpler but suffers from 3x memory consumption, data replication, image popping artifacts during stack swaps, and inconsistent sampling distances as the camera moves [S1, unofficial].
+### [Object-Aligned Slicing](6531-object-aligned-slicing.md)
+Object-aligned slicing divides the volume into slices along the principal axes (x, y, z) rather than the view direction. It uses fixed texture coordinates assigned to slice vertices and avoids per-ray intersection calculations. It is distinguished from its parent [View-Aligned Slicing](6530-view-aligned-slicing.md) by its simplicity and speed, but suffers from higher memory consumption and visual artifacts due to inconsistent sampling distances and stack swapping.
 
